# Documentation and Course Content Standards

## Japanese Educational Content

This course is designed for Japanese learners, so:

- Primary language should be Japanese for lesson content
- Technical terms should include both English and Japanese explanations
- Code comments can be in English (standard practice)
- Use clear, progressive explanations building from simple to complex

## Lesson Structure

### Unit Lessons (`unit-*/lessons/*.md`)

- Start with learning objectives
- Include practical code examples
- Provide step-by-step instructions
- End with key takeaways or summary

### Exercise Files (`exercises/*/`)

- Clear problem statements
- Provide starting code or templates when helpful
- Include expected outcomes
- Answer keys should have detailed explanations

## Content Organization

- Use consistent heading hierarchy (H1 for main topics, H2 for sections)
- Include code blocks with proper syntax highlighting using `move` language tag
- Link to relevant example projects using relative paths
- Include visual aids in the [images](mdc:unit-*/images/) directories when helpful

## Cross-References

- Link between related lessons and concepts
- Reference example projects: `[project_name](../example_projects/project_name/)`
- Connect theoretical content to practical implementations

## Code Examples in Documentation

```move
// Use proper Move syntax highlighting
public entry fun example_function(ctx: &mut TxContext) {
    // Include clear comments
}
```

## Visual Learning

- Include screenshots for UI interactions
- Use diagrams for complex concepts
- Reference images with descriptive alt text
